=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-deficiency-cause-registry
{
 "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. For every DeficiencyV2 member, WHICH retained field carries its cause and what that field must say. There is no default row: a deficiency with no row here is a registry defect and refuses. Nothing in this registry changes DeficiencyV2, NativeCause, UnresolvedEdgeKindV1, ClosedWorldV2, the public DomainDetailCode registry or any D9 class, code or exit; every carrier it names already exists and is already closed.",
 "whyItIsNeeded": "native-evidence section 10 labels its second column `Cause (nativeCause)` and then fills three of its rows with values that are not NativeCause members: `the unresolved edge classes; partial/not-attempted stage` for `resolution-incomplete`, `exports-open, exports-unknown` for `external-consumers-unknown`, and `compiler-inferred present` for `derivation-policy-unmet`. Each refuses schema validation as a nativeCause (`'computed-member-access' is not valid under any of the given schemas`), so the only representable value was null - which the NativeCause description itself defines as `a disclosure was owed and not made`. Worse, nothing compared the pair to anything: on the frozen reference a `resolution-incomplete` entry carrying the unrelated but schema-valid `lockfile-missing`, or relabelled `capability-missing`, or a `derivation-policy-unmet` with an empty derivationKinds, or an `external-consumers-unknown` over `exportsClosed: closed`, each closed a Run. A cause vocabulary that nothing derives and nothing compares leaves the disclosure optional in practice - the same argument that added the three body-language-* members, carried through to the rest of the table.",
 "whyNotMoreEnumMembers": "Adding NativeCause members was rejected on the merits, not for compatibility. `resolution-incomplete` has a genuinely SET-VALUED cause: an entry can exhibit up to all sixteen UnresolvedEdgeKindV1 classes at once, and a scalar cannot carry them, so enum members would either lose information or force an arbitrary pick - the multi-cause defect the finding names. All three rows already have closed, structured, independently DERIVED carriers in the same record: unresolvedEdgeClasses (which RC-2 already forces to equal the admitted unresolved-edge facts), ClosedWorldV2.exportsClosed, and derivationKinds. The correction is to NAME those carriers and ENFORCE the join, not to duplicate them in a scalar.",
 "nullIsNotAnEscape": "Where a row says nativeCause MUST be null, that null no longer records an unmade disclosure: the row names the field that carries the cause instead, and admission REFUSES unless that field actually carries it. A `resolution-incomplete` entry whose resolutionCompleteness claims `complete` is refused, so `null` cannot be used to say nothing.",
 "multiCauseAndPrecedence": "An entry carries ONE deficiency and simultaneously carries every carrier this registry names. What each carrier RETAINS differs, and an earlier revision wrongly generalised the strongest case to all of them by saying the scalar `loses nothing`. The accurate statement is per carrier: resolutionCompleteness.unresolvedEdgeClasses is a genuine SET and retains EVERY applicable unresolved class at once, already forced by RC-2 to equal the admitted unresolved-edge facts of the view; closedWorld and derivationKinds are independent fields that retain their own state whatever the deficiency is; but input-closure-incomplete's carrier is a SINGLE SCALAR NativeCause, and a Run can genuinely be missing several inputs at once - two absent crates, or an absent crate AND an unavailable generated file. For that row the retained cause is a SELECTED one, not the full set. See selectedScalarCauseLimitation. Which single deficiency is declared when several apply is the section 10 precedence order, applied by the owning sufficiency evaluation against a REQUIREMENT. That selection is deliberately NOT re-derived from the entry alone, and the limit is stated rather than worked around: several conditions are requirement-relative (external-consumer state matters only for a universal negative about an exported target; the confidence floor lives in the Requirement, not the entry), and RC-3 makes `coverage: complete` with `state: incomplete` and NO deficiency a valid honest entry. Forcing a deficiency from exhibited entry evidence would therefore refuse lawful Runs. What IS decidable from the entry is the direction enforced here: a DECLARED deficiency must be SUPPORTED by the entry's own committed evidence.",
 "retainedVersusPublicRoute": "Two different routes and neither substitutes for the other. RETAINED: the whole ViewEntryV3 lives inside the coverage2 record, whose payload digest and schema digest the Run commits, so every carrier named below is retained evidence a verifier can re-derive. PUBLIC: the DEFICIENCY is projected outward as its own closed DomainDetailCode - `resolution-incomplete`, `external-consumers-unknown` and `derivation-policy-unmet` are all already members - and the CAUSE travels as typed detail in the coverage2 record the host termination names by its coverageId, exactly as section 10 has always said for the nativeCause-carried rows. No new public code is added and none is needed.",
 "deficiencies": {
  "language-tier-unsupported": {
   "carrier": "entry.nativeCause",
   "nativeCause": "required",
   "allowedCauses": [
    "capability-missing"
   ],
   "rule": "The admitted universe's selected grammars do not bear the requested relation@rung. The cause is a single named state, so nativeCause carries it and must be exactly capability-missing. Already derived and enforced by the syntax capability prerequisite; stated here so one table covers every member."
  },
  "provider-unavailable": {
   "carrier": "entry.nativeCause",
   "nativeCause": "optional",
   "allowedCauses": [
    "capability-missing",
    "linker-unavailable"
   ],
   "rule": "Section 10 leaves this row's Cause cell empty, so a null is lawful and self-describing; where a more specific named state applies it is carried. No other cause may appear."
  },
  "input-closure-incomplete": {
   "carrier": "entry.nativeCause",
   "nativeCause": "required",
   "allowedCauses": [
    "body-language-owner-ambiguous",
    "body-language-owner-unenumerated",
    "body-language-ownership-missing",
    "config-flag-stripped",
    "generated-file-missing",
    "generated-file-out-of-bounds",
    "generated-output-unavailable",
    "lockfile-missing",
    "missing-dependency-source",
    "no-program-unit",
    "node-modules-outside-read-set",
    "source-replacement-outside-snapshot"
   ],
   "rule": "The one deficiency whose cause genuinely IS a single named missing input, which is why section 10 lists twelve of them and why the three body-language-* members were added here. A null refuses: this row is exactly where a null would record an unmade disclosure."
  },
  "budget-exhausted": {
   "carrier": "entry.resolutionCompleteness.stageTerminal",
   "nativeCause": "must-be-null",
   "requires": {
    "path": [
     "resolutionCompleteness",
     "stageTerminal"
    ],
    "equals": "budget-exhausted"
   },
   "rule": "Section 10 already qualifies this row `(stage terminal)`. The stage terminal is the cause and it is a closed StageTerminal value in the same entry, so nativeCause is null by design and the terminal must actually say budget-exhausted."
  },
  "confidence-floor-unmet": {
   "carrier": "none-in-entry",
   "nativeCause": "must-be-null",
   "rule": "Requirement-relative and deliberately without an entry carrier: the FLOOR lives in RequirementV2.minConfidenceMillionths and the entry carries only its own confidenceMillionths, so the entry cannot decide whether the floor was met. The comparison is the sufficiency evaluation's, and stating that limit is honest where inventing an entry-level predicate would not be."
  },
  "derivation-policy-unmet": {
   "carrier": "entry.derivationKinds",
   "relations": [
    "types"
   ],
   "relationLaw": "The OWNING law scopes this deficiency to one relation. Native section 4.7 and sufficiency_v2 apply derivationPolicy only where `rel == \"types\"` and the requirement asks for `declared-only`; there is no derivation policy for any other relation, so no other relation's entry can be short of one. An earlier revision checked the derivationKinds carrier ALONE, and a complete Run therefore admitted this declared deficiency on a `references` entry that merely carried the same array value (root diagnostic: references ADMIT, types ADMIT). An entry whose relation is outside this list cannot declare it.",
   "nativeCause": "must-be-null",
   "contains": {
    "path": [
     "derivationKinds"
    ],
    "member": "compiler-inferred"
   },
   "rule": "Section 10's Cause cell reads `compiler-inferred present`, which is a statement about derivationKinds - a closed three-member set in this same entry - and not a NativeCause. The entry must actually carry compiler-inferred among its derivation kinds.",
   "thisIsDeclarationSupportNotAnObligation": "Two different assertions, and only the first is enforced. (1) An entry that DECLARES this deficiency must be for relation `types` and must actually carry `compiler-inferred`. (2) Whether a given requirement DEMANDS the deficiency is sufficiency_v2's question against a RequirementV2, and is not derived here. `compiler-inferred` types are exact under the admitted universe and are perfectly valid under `derivationPolicy=any`; they become a deficiency only under `declared-only`. Nothing here imposes declared-only on a valid types entry, and controls hold both directions."
  },
  "resolution-incomplete": {
   "carrier": "entry.resolutionCompleteness",
   "nativeCause": "must-be-null",
   "oneOf": {
    "path": [
     "resolutionCompleteness",
     "state"
    ],
    "members": [
     "incomplete",
     "partial",
     "not-attempted"
    ]
   },
   "rule": "Section 10's Cause cell reads `the unresolved edge classes; partial/not-attempted stage`, and both halves are ResolutionCompletenessV2 fields: `state` names the stage condition and `unresolvedEdgeClasses` carries EVERY applicable class at once, already forced by RC-2 to equal the admitted unresolved-edge facts of this view. A claim of this deficiency over a `complete` or `not-applicable` resolution state is refused: that is the entry contradicting its own evidence."
  },
  "external-consumers-unknown": {
   "carrier": "entry.closedWorld.exportsClosed",
   "nativeCause": "must-be-null",
   "oneOf": {
    "path": [
     "closedWorld",
     "exportsClosed"
    ],
    "members": [
     "open",
     "unknown"
    ]
   },
   "rule": "Section 10's Cause cell reads `exports-open, exports-unknown`, which are the ClosedWorldV2 exportsClosed values `open` and `unknown` in this same entry, not NativeCause members. `closed` refuses: an entry cannot claim its consumers are unknown while its own closed-world record says they are closed."
  },
  "required-relation-missing": {
   "carrier": "none-in-entry",
   "nativeCause": "must-be-null",
   "rule": "A sufficiency verdict about a relation the view does NOT carry an entry for, so an entry can never be its own evidence. It has no entry carrier by construction and takes no cause. Kept admissible rather than refused because nothing in this contract states that a host may not record it, and inventing that refusal is not this correction's to make."
  }
 },
 "noDeficiencyNoCause": "A null deficiency requires a null nativeCause. A cause without a deficiency names why nothing went wrong, which is not a claim this record can carry.",
 "enforcedAt": "native_evidence_model admit_coverage_result_v3 - the producer boundary - and therefore again at identity-model.v3.open_run_closure, which re-runs that exact admission over the RETAINED coverage payload (native owner admission, not evaluator correctness). identity-model.v3.close_run is complete replay of evaluator3 output. Refusals are native.coverage-cause-* and are PROVIDER.PROTOCOL_VIOLATION like the other producer-boundary refusals.",
 "selectedScalarCauseLimitation": "STATED LIMITATION, not a defect to be engineered around. input-closure-incomplete retains ONE NativeCause while several missing inputs may coexist. Provenance of the selection: the producer names the cause it acted on, and Run closure checks that the named cause is lawful for the deficiency - it does not and cannot enumerate the inputs that were not named. What IS fully retained beside it is the rest of the Coverage entry and the Run's own evidence: the missing dependency sources, prepared rows and generated files are retained records in their own right, so a consumer that needs the complete set reads those rather than the scalar. No multi-cause format is introduced merely to make the prose true; the bounded source/ownership laws stay exactly as they are, and the limitation is published so no consumer reads the scalar as exhaustive.",
 "perRequirementConsumerBoundary": {
  "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. WHERE a per-requirement sufficiency_v2 outcome travels once it leaves this unit, and in WHICH vocabulary. This block adds no member to DeficiencyV2, changes no carrier row above, adds no D9 class, code, exit or public detail code, and changes no D9 projection: it names the consumer boundary that was previously unnamed.",
  "producer": "native-evidence.md section 4.6 `sufficiency_v2`, evaluated once per RequirementV2 against one NATIVE view, and therefore defined for the thirteen native fact relations only. Its FULL result is {satisfied, deficiency?, disclosures, causes}: `causes` is returned on BOTH branches - empty when satisfied - and `disclosures` may be non-empty on the FAILING branch as well. An earlier revision called the result shape closed and total as {satisfied:true,disclosures} or {satisfied:false,deficiency}; that described only the two fields a consumer projects, not the producer's output, and is corrected here.",
  "consumers": [
   {
    "record": "workflows/schemas/repair.schema.json#/$defs/EvidenceRequirement",
    "field": "deficiency",
    "carries": "the DeficiencyV2 member sufficiency_v2 returned for THIS requirement",
    "vocabulary": "urn:opensip:product-v1:workflows:common#/$defs/NativeSufficiencyDeficiency for a NATIVE-plane requirement - a generated/drift-checked mirror of #/$defs/DeficiencyV2 in its declared member order, because the workflows bundle resolves only workflows URNs and takes no cross-bundle $ref. An IMPORTED-plane requirement of the same record carries #/$defs/ImportedRequirementDeficiency instead, under its own owning law; the field admits either vocabulary and the boundary decides which one this relation may use.",
    "presenceLaw": "REQUIRED exactly when satisfied is false; FORBIDDEN when satisfied is true; `satisfied` must be an actual boolean; and an explicit null is refused in BOTH branches, because a present key with a null value is not an absent key. Schema-enforced in repair.schema.json and decided again by workflows_model.admit_evidence_requirement, which routes every violation to CONFIG.INVALID - the class the owning schema validation and admit_atom already use for a malformed input - and adds no public detail code. The two boundaries are held EQUAL on an enumerated shape table, except for the cross-plane rows: this schema deliberately leaves the authoritative relation-registry lookup to admission. Its oneOf admits either outcome vocabulary on either relation; admission decides the plane from registry membership. JSON Schema can branch on const/enum under a broader string type, but this schema does not duplicate that registry as conditionals or fetch its rows. This is the selected division between schema validation and admission, not a limitation caused by the relation type.",
    "authorityLimit": "The field is a DISCLOSURE, not a grant and not a gate input. `applicable` is false whenever any requirement is unsatisfied, the evidence authority stays the sealed Run named by evidenceRunId, and apply requires a security authorization bound to the exact repairPlanId - which every edit to this value changes."
   }
  ],
  "whyNotTheD9Vocabulary": "The field previously named workflows common#/$defs/D9Deficiency, which cannot express four of the nine members its only producer emits: derivation-policy-unmet, external-consumers-unknown, input-closure-incomplete and resolution-incomplete have no member there. Only three readings were conforming and no document chose between them: write the D9-mapped value, which is `verdict-indeterminate` for ALL FOUR and therefore collapses exactly the per-row distinction this registry exists to keep - including resolution-incomplete, which section 4.6 step 6 mandates for a universal negative under unresolvedEdgePolicy=forbid over an affected target and which is the outcome a destructive unused-code repair recipe turns on; write the sufficiency value and be schema-invalid for four of nine; or omit the optional field and drop the disclosure. The repair is to retype the ONE per-requirement field, not to widen D9Deficiency: that enum is the whole-Run and comparison-step termination vocabulary of the inherited D9 exit contract, three of its members (convergence-exhausted, baseline-recipe-unsupported, query-completeness-unmet) name outcomes no per-requirement evaluation can produce, and widening it would change a D9 vocabulary to fix a workflows record. The DomainDetailCode route does not resolve it either: that registry carries a DIFFERENT five of the nine (budget-exhausted plus the four above), so the union of the two covers all nine and neither single field does.",
  "threeDistinctThingsNotToConflate": {
   "nativeOutcome": "the DeficiencyV2 member for one requirement - what this block routes.",
   "publicD9Termination": "the class/exit/errorCode of the Run or step that carries the requirement - unchanged, still the section 10 table columns (indeterminate (3) with VERDICT.INDETERMINATE, COVERAGE.PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE or COVERAGE.BUDGET_EXHAUSTED) and still spelled with D9Deficiency where a D9 record needs a deficiency.",
   "retainedCause": "the Coverage entry's own cause carrier - entry.nativeCause or the named structured field per the `deficiencies` rows above. The requirement record carries the OUTCOME and never the carrier: several outcomes are requirement-relative (the confidence floor lives in RequirementV2; external-consumer state matters only for a universal negative about an exported target) and required-relation-missing has no entry at all, so a carrier could not be copied here even in principle.",
   "satisfiedState": "a boolean about THIS requirement, not a verdict and not a coverage state. satisfied=true may still carry section 4.6 disclosures, which are not deficiencies and are not carried by this field."
  },
  "enforcedAt": "workflows_model.admit_evidence_requirement (the repair-preview producer/consumer boundary, run before any descriptor exists, refusals native.sufficiency-outcome-* under CONFIG.INVALID), the repair.schema.json presence law, and the cross-unit parity and plane-routing controls in docs/coop/design-corrections/check-integration.py.",
  "driftIsAFailureNotAWidening": "A member added to or removed from DeficiencyV2 without the same change in the workflows mirror fails the cross-unit parity control. The mirror is never allowed to be a superset: a workflows record must not be able to state a native outcome this unit does not define.",
  "whatTheConsumerCarries": "the SATISFACTION/DEFICIENCY PROJECTION of that result: `satisfied`, and exactly one DeficiencyV2 member when it is false, chosen by the section 10 precedence. `causes` and `disclosures` are NOT projected. They are the PRODUCER RESULT ARRAYS of that evaluation and are not fields of any record here: they are not members of CoverageResultV3, and some of them could not be - the confidence floor lives in RequirementV2, the external-consumer condition is requirement-relative, and `required-relation-missing` has no Coverage entry at all. What IS retained, and what a verifier re-derives, is the evidence the evaluation READ: the `coverage2` record with its own deficiency and nativeCause carrier per section 10, and the rest of the Run's committed closure. So the accurate statement is that the projection drops nothing that was retained, and that re-deriving the full result means re-running the evaluation over that retained evidence - not reading `causes` out of a stored field. No new wire field is introduced to carry them. The projection is total in the only sense a consumer needs: a failing requirement always has exactly one reported outcome, so omitting it is never lawful.",
  "thisBlockOwnsTheNativePlaneOnly": "Repair also admits a requirement over the two IMPORTED-EVIDENCE relations, `runtime-observation` and `history-change`. They mint no fact2, carry no sourceUniverse/targetUniverse and have no Coverage entry, so sufficiency_v2 has nothing to range over and would only report `required-relation-missing`, which says nothing true about an import. Their outcomes are owned by workflows/schemas/imported-evidence.schema.json#/x-opensip-imported-requirement-law and carry a DISJOINT vocabulary. An earlier revision described sufficiency_v2 as the only defined producer of that consumer field; that was false for those two relations and is corrected here. Which plane applies is decided at ADMISSION from relation-registry membership, never from the value."
 }
}
=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-grammar-capability-registry
{
 "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. The per-language capability mapping of the bundled grammar set, published here so a consumer can reconstruct it without reading author code. syntaxClass is NOT caller-selected: a grammar row declaring a class other than the one this registry assigns to its languageId is refused at grammar-bundle admission, in both directions.",
 "source": "native_evidence_model.v2.BUNDLED_GRAMMARS (the same table the shared discovery rule uses for the grammar-only membership path) and native-evidence section 6.3.",
 "classLaw": {
  "code": "The language has the section 6.3 body spans - function, method, closure/lambda, impl item and block bodies - and a published L2/L3 normalisation table. Bears clone body identity (clones@normalized-body-hash) and the code-construct syntax relations (declares/literal/control-flow @ syntactic). Its languageId MUST be a member of foundation body-language-version.languageId, because that identifier enters the clone preimage.",
  "data-document": "A bundled grammar for a data or document format. It has no section 6.3 body spans, and this kit publishes no tokenisation, literalKind mapping or normalisation law for it. Its files are grammar-only MEMBERS - accounted, inventoried, never unsupported-file - bearing file/package/vcs-change inventory evidence under the syntax universe. It mints NO body identity, no suffix of it appears in the syntax dialect table, and its languageId MUST NOT be in body-language-version.languageId. A clone or code-construct request against it is explicitly UNAVAILABLE (language-tier-unsupported for that capability), never a complete empty clone result, which would read as a finding of no clones."
 },
 "languages": {
  "typescript": {
   "syntaxClass": "code",
   "suffixes": [
    ".cts",
    ".mts",
    ".ts",
    ".tsx"
   ],
   "capabilities": [
    "declares@syntactic",
    "literal@syntactic",
