 },
 "unbundledLanguages": "A language with no bundled grammar is not representable here at all: its files are unsupported-file with reason no-bundled-grammar, and its languageId is outside the SyntaxGrammarBundleV1 vocabulary. Adding one is a deliberate registry change, not a caller choice.",
 "futureCapability": "Data-format syntax facts (for example literal@syntactic over JSON or YAML) would require a published tokenisation and literalKind law. None exists in this kit, so the mapping above does not promise them. This is a bounded future capability, deliberately not invented here.",
 "enforcementBoundaries": "This registry is the authority at THREE boundaries, not one. (1) Grammar-bundle admission: a row's declared syntaxClass must equal the registered one, in both directions. (2) Every admitted fact2 under a syntax universe: every ANCHOR PATH must be read by a SELECTED grammar whose capability set contains that relation@rung. (3) Every requested subject-scope under a syntax universe, INCLUDING an empty view: judged on the committed examined extent, where a code capability needs at least one extent path read by a selected code grammar, and a capability no selected grammar bears is unavailable. INVENTORY CAPABILITIES (file@enumerated, package@manifest-declared, vcs-change@vcs-reported) are ALWAYS available and are never grammar-gated at boundary (2) OR boundary (3). An earlier revision stated that exemption only inside boundary (3), and boundary (2) read as an unconditional every-anchor rule; a literal implementer therefore refused the very inventory facts boundary (3) had just promised were unconditionally available, which made an inventoried .py path inventoriable and unrepresentable at the same time. The exemption belongs to both because it is a property of the RELATION, not of the boundary: an inventory fact is not read by a grammar at all - it is the snapshot inventory row, the manifest declaration or the VCS observation - so there is no grammar for it to be gated on. Independently of this exemption the relation registry anchorLaw closes the inventory class at ZERO anchors, so an inventory fact names no anchor path for boundary (2) to test in the first place; the two laws agree and neither depends on the other. An earlier revision enforced only (1), which constrains what a bundle says about itself and not what a Run claims: Markdown declares facts, complete empty declares/clones Coverage and references@resolved-binding under resolutionAttempted=false all admitted. None of the three consults a claimed coverage value, a caller-declared class, or whether facts exist.",
 "unavailableRequestDisclosure": "A well-formed request for a capability this universe cannot serve is DISCLOSED, not answered and not refused outright: coverage `unknown`, deficiency `language-tier-unsupported`, nativeCause `capability-missing` - existing vocabulary, mapped in native-evidence section 10. A false `complete`, a wrong or null cause and a wrong deficiency each refuse with their own typed reason. The capability is retained: a data-grammar inventory Run and a code-grammar declares/clones Run still close with a genuine complete and no deficiency.",
 "selectionMatters": "An UNSELECTED grammar lends no capability. Selection is committed in the universe identity, so a bundle that ships a grammar the universe did not select cannot make its capabilities available.",
 "inventoryIsNotGrammarGated": "Normative. A snapshot inventories every file it contains, including ones no bundled grammar reads, so requiring a grammar per inventory row would silently narrow the promise this registry makes. The three inventory capabilities are therefore exempt at every boundary this registry owns, on every path - unsupported suffix, extensionless, data-document and code alike - and a `vcs-change` on a `deleted` path that is not in the snapshot at all. This is an exemption from GRAMMAR OWNERSHIP only. It does not exempt an inventory fact from its relation's snapshot joins (path in the inventory; for `file` also the content digest, the byte length and custody of the retained bytes), from own-scope attribution, or from the anchorLaw; and it does not make a CODE fact admissible merely because some other anchor of the same Run is on a supported path."
}
=== coop/design-corrections/native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-config-node-kind-law
{
 "standing": "Normative and CLOSED. THE authority for TypeScriptConfigGraphV1 node `kind`. The schema enum publishes the three admissible values; this law publishes which one each node's path takes, which the prose previously delegated to `the schema and native admission` while neither stated it. It adds no enum member, no field and no record.",
 "selector": "#/$defs/TypeScriptConfigGraphV1/properties/nodes/items/properties/kind",
 "appliesTo": "EVERY node of the retained graph - the selected entry AND every non-entry node reached through `extendsResolved`. There is no entry-only rule and no default for the rest. A non-entry node's kind affects no derived value (see derivedValueScope), which is exactly WHY it needed a published rule: nothing else pins it and it is still committed to the identity.",
 "rule": "kind is a total function of `node.path` alone. Take the BASENAME - the final `/`-separated segment of the path - and compare it BYTE-EXACTLY against the closed table below. A match takes the table's value; anything else is `other`. Nothing else is consulted: not the file's content, not its position in the graph, not whether it is the entry, not the directory, and not any caller assertion.",
 "basenames": {
  "tsconfig.json": "tsconfig",
  "jsconfig.json": "jsconfig"
 },
 "otherwise": "other",
 "comparison": "EXACT and case-SENSITIVE over the whole basename, on the UTF-8 bytes of the CanonicalPath as retained. No prefix, suffix, glob or stem matching; no case folding; no Unicode normalization beyond what CanonicalPath already requires; no extension-only test. `TSConfig.json`, `.tsconfig.json` and `tsconfig.jsonc` are each `other`, and a host that lowercases the basename on a case-insensitive filesystem before comparing has produced a different graph and a different identity.",
 "examples": [
  {
   "path": "tsconfig.json",
   "kind": "tsconfig",
   "note": "the ordinary entry"
  },
  {
   "path": "jsconfig.json",
   "kind": "jsconfig",
   "note": "a jsconfig entry stays a jsconfig program even when its bases are not"
  },
  {
   "path": "packages/web/tsconfig.json",
   "kind": "tsconfig",
   "note": "NON-ENTRY, reached as a base: the basename decides, the depth does not"
  },
  {
